 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Some ground can lift a whole house, then drop it again. No earthquake needed.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Certain clays soak up water like a sponge and swell.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In a wet spring, the clay grows and pushes the house up.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In a dry summer, it shrinks and cracks, and the house drops.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But never evenly. So the foundation bends, and the walls crack.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In the US, swelling clay causes billions of dollars of damage every year.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The fix: piers that reach down below the layer that swells and shrinks.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And in a drought, some owners even water the soil around the foundation, to keep it steady.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "New cracks after a dry summer? Look down. Now you know.",
   "pausa": 0.2
  }
 ],
